# Private paper route

## 1. Scientific objective and author claim

The paper uses electronic-structure calculations to show that B-functionalization of BN-anthracenes changes frontier-orbital energies and spatial distribution. PBNA is the B-phenyl reference; the authors compare it with TPA- and TPE-functionalized analogues.

## 2. System and model boundary

The computational systems are isolated neutral closed-shell molecules. PBNA, TPA-PBNA, TPA-BNA, TPE-PBNA and TPE-BNA are treated as gas-phase molecular systems with no explicit solvent, crystal packing, counterion or excited-state dynamics. SI Table S12 supplies Cartesian starting geometries; the main paper identifies the frontier-orbital comparison.

## 3. Authors' implemented computational route

| Step | Purpose | Input | Method/software | Key parameters | Output | Source evidence |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Build molecular models | SI Cartesian coordinates | Gaussian 09 D.01 | neutral singlet molecular calculations | starting geometries | ev_doc_c9e5bb9d4f0b_000087_2504149821a9 |
| 2 | Optimize geometries | starting geometries | Gaussian 09 D.01 | B3LYP/6-31G(d) | optimized structures | ev_doc_c9e5bb9d4f0b_000085_0c03f8f9bc81 |
| 3 | Compute electronic properties and thermochemistry | optimized structures | Gaussian 09 D.01 | B3LYP/6-31G*; orbital energies and thermochemical analysis | HOMO/LUMO, E, ZPE, G, S | ev_doc_1cde5e8ebc83_000180_9db56b78cdc7; ev_doc_c9e5bb9d4f0b_000086_667f16b002a8 |
| 4 | Interpret substituent effects | computed orbital maps and energies | comparison in paper | PBNA versus four functionalized compounds | frontier-orbital localization and energy-shift interpretation | ev_doc_1cde5e8ebc83_000180_9db56b78cdc7 |

## 4. Validation and analysis protocol

The authors use optimized molecular structures and report orbital maps/levels in Fig. 3. The SI reports electronic energy, ZPE, Gibbs free energy and entropy in Table S11. A reproduction should verify that the optimized structure is a stationary minimum with no imaginary frequency and should report the full calculation identity, units and any convergence or model-sensitivity limitations.

## 5. Private reference results

For PBNA, the paper reports HOMO −5.62 eV and LUMO −1.71 eV (Fig. 3 discussion). SI Table S11 reports E −1087.14451390 au, ZPE 0.410542 au, G −1086.786988 au and S 161.102 cal mol−1 K−1 at B3LYP/6-31G*. These are evaluator-only targets.

## 6. Limitations and interpretation boundaries

The reported orbital energies are method- and geometry-dependent one-electron quantities, not experimental ionization potentials/electron affinities. The SI text contains an apparent anomalous TPE-BNA Gibbs value; this benchmark avoids that ambiguity by scoring only PBNA. The source does not establish solvent, temperature, conformer ensemble, or uncertainty intervals for the isolated-molecule calculation.
